================================================================================
TEST T44967 -- CLI show mirror interface direction set to transmit
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 08:51-08:56 NZ (box local; both device clocks were set to box
          local time before the run, so device and host stamps are directly comparable)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-5316, objective "set a port mirror's direction to transmit and confirm,
          from the CLI and on the wire, that only transmitted traffic is copied", 4 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (x960-tb277.rel, build Mon Sep 21 11:25:03 UTC 2026)
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- `direction transmit` is reported correctly by both show commands and
         copies ONLY the frames the DUT transmits out of the monitored port.
      command accepted on destination .... yes      (entered on port1.0.10 naming port1.0.48)
      show mirror ....................... correct  (dest port1.0.10, transmit, src port1.0.48)
      show mirror interface <src> ....... correct  (keys on the MONITORED port)
      running-config .................... present  (mirror interface ... direction transmit)
      egress frames copied .............. 6        (echo replies seen at the destination)
      ingress frames copied ............. 0        (echo requests correctly NOT copied)
      forwarding undisturbed ............ yes      (ping 6/6, 0% loss while mirroring)
      removal ........................... clean    (config and dataplane both)
CAVEAT:  This case configured the FIRST mirror on a port that had never been a mirror
         destination, which takes effect immediately. Later cases in this group found that a
         mirror RE-APPLIED to a port that has previously held one copies nothing until the
         destination port is bounced, and that an in-place `direction` change is silently
         ignored. Neither affects this result, but it means `show mirror` alone is not
         evidence of mirroring on this platform -- every verdict here rests on captured frames.

