STEP 0 -- baseline, no mirror configured                                  done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                            <-- empty, no mirror exists
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'   (14 s) while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  0 packets captured
  0 packets received by filter
  0 packets dropped by kernel
host# ping -c 6 -i 0.5 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss, time 2578ms
  <-- traffic flows end to end, and the destination port sees none of it. Anything eth3
      captures later is therefore attributable to the mirror.

STEP 1 -- "set the mirror direction to transmit and read it back"         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the command is entered on the DESTINATION port and names the SOURCE port.
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction transmit
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10         <-- destination
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: transmit                <-- direction as set
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48           <-- source as named
x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.48
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: transmit
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48           <-- the command keys on the MONITORED port and
                                                returns the same record
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   mirror interface port1.0.48 direction transmit
  => STEP 1 PASS: the command is accepted, both show forms report the destination, the
     direction and the monitored port correctly, and the line is in the running-config.

STEP 2 -- "confirm on the wire that only transmitted traffic is copied"   PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  08:55:01.300457 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 1, length 64
  08:55:01.830767 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 2, length 64
  08:55:02.342621 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 3, length 64
  08:55:02.854758 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 4, length 64
  08:55:03.366611 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 5, length 64
  08:55:03.878738 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 6, length 64
  6 packets captured
  6 packets received by filter               <-- six frames, all one direction
  0 packets dropped by kernel
host# ping -c 6 -i 0.5 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  counted: echo REQUESTS (what the DUT RECEIVES on port1.0.48) = 0
           echo REPLIES  (what the DUT TRANSMITS out of port1.0.48) = 6
  => STEP 2 PASS: six replies and zero requests. The replies are the frames the DUT transmits
     out of the monitored port; the requests are the ones it receives on it. Only the
     transmitted direction was copied, which is exactly what `direction transmit` means. The
     absence of the other direction is the discriminator, not an absence of error. Forwarding
     was undisturbed -- the ping passed 6/6 while mirroring.

STEP 3 -- "remove the mirror"                                             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.48
  % port1.0.10 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
                                            <-- ADVISORY, not a rejection: the command
                                                succeeded and the prompt advanced
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                            <-- empty
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access                   <-- mirror line gone, plain access port again
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  0 packets captured
  0 packets received by filter
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  => STEP 3 PASS: the `no` form is accepted, the line is gone from the running-config, and the
     identical flow that produced six mirrored frames now produces none while still passing
     end to end. Removal is complete in both the configuration and the dataplane.
